# Bridge: snapshot records -> schema.org, the part plain RDFS can say.
#
# Pragmatic route: a record labelled :Movie is read as a movie. The snapshot
# spec keeps "record != thing" (SPEC §1); a lifting step would mint separate
# IRIs for the things. Here the record IRI stands for the thing.

@prefix rdf:    <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix rdfs:   <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix lpg:    <https://example.org/lpg#> .
@prefix l:      <http://neo4j.example/movies/label/> .
@prefix t:      <http://neo4j.example/movies/type/> .
@prefix p:      <http://neo4j.example/movies/prop/> .
@prefix schema: <https://schema.org/> .

# Labels and relationship types act as classes (SPEC §9.1, RDFS fallback, rule rdfs7).
lpg:label rdfs:subPropertyOf rdf:type .
lpg:type  rdfs:subPropertyOf rdf:type .

l:Movie  rdfs:subClassOf schema:Movie .
l:Person rdfs:subClassOf schema:Person .

# Relationships are resources: the ACTED_IN record IS the PerformanceRole,
# the REVIEWED record IS the Review.
t:ACTED_IN rdfs:subClassOf schema:PerformanceRole .
t:REVIEWED rdfs:subClassOf schema:Review .

# Property keys that already mean a schema.org property.
p:title   rdfs:subPropertyOf schema:name .
p:name    rdfs:subPropertyOf schema:name .
p:tagline rdfs:subPropertyOf schema:description .
p:summary rdfs:subPropertyOf schema:reviewBody .
